sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: docs/reference/feature-flow.md section 6, one Swamp workflow per Gateway release, manual or automatic.
  %% A failed check switches back; migrations are expand and contract. docs/reference/gateway-recovery.md#release-as-a-swamp-run.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: gateway:release:deploy
  %% kind: gateway.release
  %% behavior name: ansible-gateway-release-<phase>-209 (fixed)
  autonumber
  %% lane TR icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane AC icon=workflow pill=child tone=green
  %% lane GN icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant TR as CLI<br/>orbit · MCP
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant AC as Activate run<br/>child · new commit
  participant GN as Gateway Node<br/>Node 14
  TR->>GW: gateway:release:deploy<br/>full sha
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit gateway:release:deploy <full sha>`; the automatic tick admits the newest green commit the same way
  Note over GW: Admit the release<br/>verify agent pin · web build
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the full commit; no other release queued or running
  %%> - the agent pin passes its Sigstore check; CI published the commit's web build
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a running release record; the pin, artifact, Horizon unit and SSH targets frozen as inputs
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-gateway-release
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - at run start, mints one read token (contents, actions) into the vault
  GW-->>TR: 202 Accepted<br/>running release record
  SW->>SW: Write the intent<br/>ansible-input
  SW->>SW: Write the playbook input<br/>token · inventory
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the admitted intent, the read token and the pinned host key of the Gateway Node into a private run folder on Node 209
  %%> - every phase below is one Swamp step that runs one tagged part of `operation-gateway-release.yml` against the Gateway Node over SSH
  SW->>GN: Build the release<br/>fetch · clone · REVISION
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fetches the exact commit into `shared/orbit.git` when it is missing; the token reaches git through a private file, removed after the fetch
  %%> - refuses a commit that does not descend from the current release
  %%> - clones the commit from the shared repository, links the shared env and storage, installs dependencies with `community.general.composer`, writes the agent manifest
  %%> - seals the release with `REVISION` and a cached config; a sealed release of the commit is reused
  %%> - prints the grants the new code renders
  SW->>GN: Install the web build<br/>artifact · digest · safe extract
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - downloads the CI artifact, checks its SHA-256, extracts it with safe names only and publishes it by one rename
  SW->>GN: Snapshot the database<br/>VACUUM INTO
  SW->>GN: Migrate<br/>artisan migrate --isolated
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - from here until the switch, the release pauses with the old release live
  Note over SW: Load Swamp on Node 209<br/>operation-gateway-swamp-load
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the load child: checkout, grants the new release rendered, serve reload
  Note over SW: Hand over the release<br/>gateway-release-activate
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the child runs from the definition the load installed, so the switch runs the new commit's steps
  AC->>AC: Write the playbook input<br/>intent · inventory
  AC->>GN: Drain Horizon<br/>ansible.builtin.systemd · stop
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - waits until the unit's whole control group is empty, as long as the drain allows; Horizon's jobs open SSH connections of their own, so no shared connection master stays in the group, and the wait covers any other process a job leaves
  %%> - refuses to stop a unit that lacks the stop policy (`KillMode=mixed`, `SendSIGKILL=no`), because it would kill a running job
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the run fails with `gateway.release_horizon_unit_stale` for a unit without the policy, or `gateway.release_horizon_not_drained` when the unit did not stop in time; Horizon is started again below
  AC->>GN: Switch the release<br/>link · FPM reload · restarts
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - requires a sealed release; prints the cleanup generations before the switch
  %%> - switches the application link and the web link with `ansible.builtin.file` (a temporary link and a rename), reloads PHP-FPM, restarts each running service whose working directory is under the application
  AC->>GN: Verify the Gateway<br/>uri · /up · status version
  opt the check failed
    AC->>GN: Switch back<br/>same phase · previous release
  end
  AC->>GN: Start Horizon<br/>always · also after a failure
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the release drained Horizon, so every end of the run leaves it running: the old release after a failed drain or a switch back, the new one after a verified switch
  %%> - starting a running unit changes nothing
  AC->>GN: Resume document cleanup<br/>best effort
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the scheduler restart paused the release cleanups; one that stays paused never fails the release
  AC->>GN: Prune old releases<br/>best effort
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps the live, previous and newest sealed releases and their web builds; removes the rest with their commit pins and worktree records
  AC->>SW: Re-seed Swamp cache<br/>best effort
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a start cache older than the Swamp binary adds about 0.4 s to every dispatch; root owns the cache, so the phase removes a stale one and seeds it again as root
  opt the load or hand-over failed
    SW->>GN: Render the previous grants<br/>previous release code
    Note over SW: Restore Swamp on Node 209<br/>operation-gateway-swamp-load
    %%> Why:
    %%> - Swamp and the Gateway switch together: a failed load or hand-over leaves Swamp on the new commit while the Gateway runs the old release, so a workflow could expect an intent field the Gateway does not send yet
    %%> - the previous release renders its own grants; the load child checks out its commit and reloads serve
    %%> - a restore that fails never hides the release failure
  end
  SW->>SW: Remove the playbook input<br/>cleanup
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - fetching takes the one commit (`git fetch --no-tags`), not `ansible.builtin.git`, which would mirror every branch and tag of the repository on each release
  %%> - Horizon starts after the check, not at the switch; the check does not depend on it
  %%> - the web build and the snapshot run side by side; the migration waits for both
  %%> - the release steps are Ansible phases, one Swamp step each, like `node:role:add`; no script file is shipped to the Gateway Node or read from a release
  %%> - the build is a clone of the commit from the shared repository (`ansible.builtin.git`), not a git worktree; the clone borrows its objects from the shared repository, which `refs/orbit/<sha>` keeps alive
  %%> - the restore after a failed load or hand-over keeps Swamp and the Gateway on the same commit; it is the only step besides the release's own load that moves 209's checkout
  %%> - `bin/gateway-rollback` stays a small script: an operator needs it when Swamp or Node 209 is down, and the release survives a Swamp outage
  alt every step succeeded
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record verified<br/>fleet rollout follows
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the Gateway queues one `fleet.rollout` admission for the Nodes behind this commit (`fleet:rollout`)
  else a step before Migrate fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    Note over GW: Mark failed<br/>nothing changed · rerun safe
  else the check failed, switched back
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    Note over GW: Record switched back<br/>alert · next commit releases
  else another step from Migrate on fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    Note over GW: Pause automatic releases<br/>snapshot named · alert
  end
